# Disagreements between the gate's reading of an npm command line and npm's own that the oracle
# (tests/live/npm_option_oracle.sh, SAFE_ORACLE_GATE=1) finds and that are LISTED, not closed: one extended regular
# expression per line, matched against the oracle's `DISAGREE kind: <arguments>  (<what>)` line, each with the reason it
# stays. Every other disagreement the oracle has found is closed.

# 1. `npm exec` with a call string (--call, -c) AND a positional package: the gate audits the positional, npm fetches
#    nothing. Every reference (8.1.0 to 12.2.1) refuses the combination with its usage error before any fetch
#    (`if (call && args.length)` in lib/exec.js / lib/commands/exec.js), so this is an over-audit, never a miss. It is kept
#    on purpose: the gate audits what a command line names as a package wherever it could be one, so that a later npm that
#    accepts the combination cannot fetch an unaudited name. Reproduction: `npm exec --call=true cowsay` audits cowsay.
#    Only package-extra lines of exec/x command lines that carry --call or -c as a word of its own, and for which npm
#    fetches nothing, are listed (an option that merely begins with -c, such as --color or --cache, does not match).
^DISAGREE package-extra: (exec|x)\\ (.*\\ )?(--call|-c)(=[^\\ ]*)?(\\ .*)?  \(audited '[^']*', which npm does not fetch \(npm fetches: nothing\)\)$
